<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Scrovegni Chapel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1305</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Arena Chapel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Scrovegni Chapel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> was built for the banker Enrico Scrovegni and consecrated in 1305. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giotto">Giotto</a> covered its interior with a fresco cycle of the lives of the Virgin and Christ and the Last Judgement, under a deep blue vault. It is one of the most important works of Western painting and part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/scrovegni-chapel.jpg" alt="Scrovegni Chapel" width="320" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zairon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Padova_Cappella_degli_Scrovegni_Innen_Langhaus_West_5.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The chapel stands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> beside the Augustinian monastery of the Eremitani. It is called the Arena Chapel because the banker Enrico Scrovegni built it on land that abutted the site of a Roman arena, which he bought from Manfredo Dalesmanini, with a palace beside it. The chapel was meant as the family's private oratory and as a funerary monument for himself and his wife, Jacopina d'Este. The land was bought on 6 February 1300. It was dedicated to Santa Maria della Carità on 25 March 1303, the Feast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annunciation">Annunciation</a>, and received its definitive consecration on 25 March 1305. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giotto">Giotto</a>'s work falls between those two dates.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giotto">Giotto</a> was then about 36 to 38 years old and had a team of about 40 collaborators. They calculated that 625 working days were needed, a working day being the portion of fresco that could be painted before the plaster dried. In January 1305 the friars of the Eremitani complained to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> that Scrovegni was turning his oratory into a church with a bell tower, in competition with them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giotto">Giotto</a> covered every interior surface. The nave is 20.88 metres long, 8.41 wide and 12.65 high. The cycle runs in tiers. At the top are the stories of Joachim and Anne on the south wall and of Mary on the north, then the Life of Christ in the middle tier, and the Passion and Resurrection below, ending with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pentecost">Pentecost</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annunciation">Annunciation</a> and the Visitation stand on the triumphal arch, and the Last Judgement fills the entrance wall. Along the bottom run fourteen grisaille personifications, the Vices on the north wall and the Virtues on the south.</p><p>The vault is painted as a blue sky studded with eight-pointed stars. Its tondos hold the seven great prophets of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-testament">Old Testament</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a>, with two suns showing God and the Madonna and Child.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The frescoes are regarded as an important masterpiece of Western art and as the beginning of a revolution in mural painting that influenced technique, style and content for a century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giotto">Giotto</a>'s innovation lies in the monumentality of his forms and the clarity of his compositions. In 2021 the chapel became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> of the 14th-century fresco cycles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>. Marcel Proust refers to Giotto's Charity in his novel, and Stanley Spencer based the scale and narrative sequence of the Sandham Memorial Chapel at Burghclere on it.</p><p>The palace was demolished in 1827, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> bought the chapel in 1881. A full restoration began in June 2001, and the chapel reopened in March 2002. Visitors now wait fifteen minutes in a technological chamber that lowers their humidity and filters dust.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Scrovegni Chapel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The original ceiling, by Pier Matteo d'Amelia, showed stars over a blue background, like the <b>Arena Chapel</b> at Padua decorated by Giotto.</blockquote><a href="/work/sistine-chapel-ceiling">Sistine Chapel ceiling · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Afterwards the commune flourished: the basilica of Saint Anthony was built, Giotto painted the <b>Scrovegni Chapel</b> around 1305, and the scholar Albertino Mussato revived classical Latin drama.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-padua">Commune of Padua</a></li></ul></section>
